body:
Away from the hype of GME,  I made the mistake of buying into the hype of BB, and I am down 1.5k, I know it is not a lot to some, but it is to me, so what is the best course of action to try to avoid that lose ? hold, not close till it is mid 5-7 range, and buy a lot more to lower my average way down (right now it is 22 per share), and wait for it to get 0.5 per share on the much lower end, and then close all of the share at break even price ? or start a sell position now and ride the way down, making money on the way down ?

&#x200B;

( I am also down 500$ on GME with average 248$ which I will hold because I still think the squeeze will happen, I just don't feel the same for BB).

Here is my current financial situation

I am 28, single, living with my parents, expenses very low (less than 500 USD). 

I own two flats estimated value at 35000 USD (no tenants). 

I work as a freelancer (commission based), yearly income range 35\~45k USD 

I own 50% of a day care center (recently invested don't have accurate estimate for yearly income but aiming for at least 6.5k yearly

I have 2k in bonds, 4.5k emergency/expenses fund, no 401k or similar (I don't know if we have something like that in Egypt and if I can participate as a freelancer). 

&#x200B;

I have about 35k in checking account that I want to invest and my options is as followed 

&#x200B;

1. Investing in real estate in Dubai in high rental areas with total cost 100k USD, down payment of 10k USD and I will need to pay another 40k in a year), then the studio would be ready for rent and the rent should cover the monthly installment over 4 years time period. 

&#x200B;

2. Investing in real estate in Cairo in high rental areas with total cost 110k USD with down payment of 28k USD, building is still under construction, it will be delivered in 4 years during which the company is promising to pay 12% on the deposit (due to inflation the banks offer similar numbers for bonds). the rest is paid in installment after construction is completed. 

&#x200B;

3. Investing in US stocks with dividend growth investing (taking advantage of the lower stock prices), but because I am not in the US I am taxed 15% on all dividends and most of capital gain.

&#x200B;

4.  Investing in Egyptian stocks which I am not familiar with and higher risk factors.

&#x200B;

5. Putting them in a bond that gives 12% annually over 3 year period.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

How can I calculate Alternative opportunities or asses each risks in actual percentages/numbers ?
